    <node id="Key Terms">
      <data key="d0">Key Terms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Key Terms are important definitions and concepts that are essential for understanding Agile project management and user stories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Title">
      <data key="d0">Title</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Title is a brief and descriptive name for a user story that summarizes the functionality or feature being implemented.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Description">
      <data key="d0">Description</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Description provides a detailed explanation of a user story, including the problem it addresses and the value it provides to the user.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DevOps">
      <data key="d0">DevOps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DevOps is a set of practices that combines software development (Dev) and IT operations (Ops) to shorten development life cycles and deliver high-quality software.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Story Points">
      <data key="d0">Story Points</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Story Points are a numerical estimate reflecting the complexity, effort, and time required to complete a user story, often using a relative scale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Meetings and Refinement Sessions">
      <data key="d0">Meetings and Refinement Sessions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Meetings and Refinement Sessions are collaborative discussions in Agile that help clarify user stories and requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roles &amp; Responsibilities">
      <data key="d0">Roles &amp; Responsibilities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Roles &amp; Responsibilities outline the specific duties and expectations assigned to individuals within a team or organization.&lt;SEP&gt;Roles &amp; Responsibilities outline the specific duties and functions of team members within an Agile project.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scrum Master">
      <data key="d0">Scrum Master</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Scrum Master facilitates the Scrum process, ensuring adherence to Agile principles and practices while removing impediments and fostering collaboration among team members.&lt;SEP&gt;The Scrum Master is a role within Agile that facilitates the Scrum process, ensuring that the team adheres to Agile principles and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAP 101">
      <data key="d0">RAP 101</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">RAP 101 is a reference to a training or informational session focused on the RAP (Rapid Assessment Process) methodology in project management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="WBS">
      <data key="d0">WBS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">WBS (Work Breakdown Structure) is a hierarchical decomposition of a project into smaller, manageable components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="User Interface for Operators">
      <data key="d0">User Interface for Operators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The User Interface for Operators refers to the design and functionality tailored for operators to interact with a system or application.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MES RIS 102.4">
      <data key="d0">MES RIS 102.4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MES RIS 102.4 is a document that provides guidelines for defining business process scenarios in Manufacturing Execution Systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f71e5a295481a80d1839f4ba2e4c08a</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Product Owner">
      <data key="d0">Product Owner</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Product Owner represents stakeholders and is responsible for defining the product backlog, prioritizing features, and ensuring the team delivers value to the customer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Senior Developer/Engineer">
      <data key="d0">Senior Developer/Engineer</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Senior Developer/Engineer is responsible for implementing complex features, mentoring junior developers, and contributing to code quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ADO">
      <data key="d0">ADO</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ADO (Azure DevOps) is a platform used for managing software development projects, including user stories and work items.&lt;SEP&gt;ADO refers to Azure DevOps, a cloud-based service for collaboration on software development projects, including version control and project management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce656892960bdf19bad87a40627f43b9</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Architectural Considerations">
      <data key="d0">Architectural Considerations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Architectural considerations refer to the fundamental design principles and frameworks that guide the development of systems and applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Models">
      <data key="d0">Data Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data models are abstract representations of the data structures that dictate how data is stored, organized, and manipulated within a system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APIs">
      <data key="d0">APIs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">APIs (Application Programming Interfaces) are sets of rules and protocols for building and interacting with software applications, enabling communication between different systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Technical Requirements">
      <data key="d0">Technical Requirements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Technical requirements define the specifications and conditions that must be met for a system to function effectively and meet user needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complexity Estimate">
      <data key="d0">Complexity Estimate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A complexity estimate is a numerical value that reflects the difficulty and effort required to complete a user story, often using scales like Fibonacci numbers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tags">
      <data key="d0">Tags</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tags are keywords or labels used to categorize and organize user stories, aiding in filtering and searching within project management systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Suppliers">
      <data key="d0">Suppliers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Suppliers are entities that provide the necessary inputs for processes, which can include people, organizations, or systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Suppliers are the teams or external entities providing inputs necessary for the process, influencing its efficiency and effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce656892960bdf19bad87a40627f43b9</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inputs">
      <data key="d0">Inputs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inputs are the resources, materials, or information needed to perform a process, essential for achieving desired outputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Inputs refer to the various components or materials required to initiate a process, playing a crucial role in the SIPOC framework.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce656892960bdf19bad87a40627f43b9</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Process">
      <data key="d0">Process</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Process denotes the series of actions or steps taken to achieve a particular end in project management, as outlined in the SIPOC model.&lt;SEP&gt;Process refers to the series of steps or activities carried out to transform inputs into outputs in a systematic way.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce656892960bdf19bad87a40627f43b9</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Outputs">
      <data key="d0">Outputs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Outputs are the products, services, or results generated by a process, representing the end goal of the activities performed.&lt;SEP&gt;Outputs are the results or products generated by a process, essential for assessing the effectiveness of the workflow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce656892960bdf19bad87a40627f43b9</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Customers">
      <data key="d0">Customers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Customers are the end-users or beneficiaries of the outputs from a process, whose needs and satisfaction are central to the development effort.&lt;SEP&gt;Customers are the end-users or stakeholders who benefit from the outputs of a process, making their needs central to project planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d120b178baf3ca897f1a7c5fe4186ea2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce656892960bdf19bad87a40627f43b9</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Work Breakdown Schedule">
      <data key="d0">Work Breakdown Schedule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Work Breakdown Schedule (WBS) is a structured framework that breaks down project scope into manageable components, enhancing delivery timelines and resource allocation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-930089a1f69bb72daa22a86ca0b5e2d8</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Server Structure">
      <data key="d0">Server Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Server Structure refers to the technical layout and requirements needed for executing a project, including development, quality assurance, and production environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-930089a1f69bb72daa22a86ca0b5e2d8</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Code Management">
      <data key="d0">Code Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Code Management involves strategies for handling source code, including version control and deployment pipelines, crucial for project execution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-930089a1f69bb72daa22a86ca0b5e2d8</data>
      <data key="d4">RAPs for MES - Development.pdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="System Integration">
